Project C-137: Remote Access Tool (RAT)
Project C-137 is a Remote Access Tool designed for monitoring and controlling remote systems. It consists of a Flask-based server (rat_server.py) and a Python client (rat_client.py). The server provides a web interface for managing connected clients, issuing commands, and exfiltrating data such as system info, webcam captures, screenshots, screen streams, microphone recordings, and keylogs.
Note: This tool is intended for educational purposes and authorized testing only. Unauthorized use on systems without explicit permission is illegal and unethical.
Features

Client Management: View and manage connected clients with details like IP, location, and system status.
Command Execution: Send commands to clients (e.g., whoami, screenshot, webcam).
Data Exfiltration:
System and network usage graphs.
Webcam captures, screenshots, and screen streaming.
Microphone recordings.
Keylogging.


File Upload/Download: Upload files to clients or download files from them.
Authentication: Secure login page to prevent unauthorized access.
Cross-Platform: Client can be compiled for both Windows and Linux.

Project Structure
project-c137/
│
├── rat_server.py         # Flask server for managing clients and serving the web interface
├── rat_client.py         # Client script to be run on target systems
├── templates/
│   ├── index.html        # Main dashboard template
│   └── login.html        # Login page template
├── uploads/              # Directory for storing uploaded files and exfiltrated data
├── downloads/            # Directory for storing files downloaded from clients
├── .env                  # Environment variables for server configuration
└── README.md             # Project documentation

Prerequisites

Python 3.8 or higher
A web browser for accessing the dashboard
System dependencies (for client):
Windows: Ensure webcam, microphone, and screen capture permissions are enabled.
Linux: Install libopencv-dev, portaudio19-dev, and alsa-utils for media capture.



Installation
1. Clone the Repository
git clone https://github.com/yourusername/project-c137.git
cd project-c137

2. Set Up a Virtual Environment
python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate  # On Windows: venv\Scripts\activate

3. Install Dependencies
pip install -r requirements.txt

Note: Create a requirements.txt file with the following dependencies:
Flask==2.3.3
Flask-SocketIO==5.3.6
python-dotenv==1.0.1
socketio-client==0.5.7.2
psutil==6.0.0
pyautogui==0.9.54
opencv-python==4.10.0.84
pyaudio==0.2.14
Pillow==10.4.0
pynput==1.7.7

4. Configure Environment Variables
Create a .env file in the project root with the following:
AUTH_USERNAME=your_username
AUTH_PASSWORD=your_password
SECRET_KEY=your_secret_key


Replace your_username and your_password with secure credentials.
Generate a secure SECRET_KEY (e.g., using python -c "import os; print(os.urandom(24).hex())").

5. Run the Server
python rat_server.py


Access the dashboard at http://localhost:5000.
Log in using the credentials from .env.

Client Setup
1. Compile the Client
The client script (rat_client.py) can be compiled into standalone executables for Windows and Linux using PyInstaller.
On Windows
pyinstaller --onefile --noconsole --name rat_client rat_client.py


Output: dist/rat_client.exe
The --noconsole flag ensures the client runs silently without a command prompt window.

On Linux
pyinstaller --onefile --name rat_client rat_client.py
chmod +x dist/rat_client


Output: dist/rat_client

2. Run the Client

Copy the compiled client to the target system.
Run the executable:
Windows: Double-click rat_client.exe or run via command line.
Linux: ./rat_client


The client will connect to the server and appear in the dashboard.

3. Ensure Stealth (Optional)

The client logs to rat_client.log instead of the console for stealth.
Persistence:
Windows: The client sets itself to run on startup via the Windows Registry.
Linux: Use a systemd service (see rat_client.py comments for setup).



Usage

Access the Dashboard:

Open http://localhost:5000 in your browser.
Log in with your credentials.


Manage Clients:

View connected clients in the dashboard.
Select a client to monitor system/network usage, capture media, or send commands.


Send Commands:

Use the command input to send commands like webcam, screenshot, mic, or custom shell commands.
Quick actions (e.g., keylog, start_stream) are available as buttons.


Exfiltrate Data:

View system info, network usage, webcam captures, screenshots, screen streams, and keylogs in the dashboard.
Microphone recordings are playable in the browser.


File Operations:

Upload files to clients via the upload button.
Download files from clients using the download <filename> command.



Security Considerations

HTTPS: In production, deploy the server with HTTPS to encrypt traffic.
Strong Credentials: Use strong, unique values for AUTH_USERNAME and AUTH_PASSWORD.
Firewall: Restrict server access to trusted IPs.
Client Stealth: The client runs silently and logs to a file, but ensure legal and ethical use.
Data Storage: Exfiltrated data is stored in uploads/. Secure this directory and delete sensitive data after use.

Troubleshooting

Client Not Connecting:
Ensure the server is running and accessible (check firewall and port 5000).
Verify the client’s network connectivity to the server.


Media Capture Issues:
Windows: Check permissions for webcam/microphone.
Linux: Install required libraries (libopencv-dev, portaudio19-dev) and ensure ALSA is configured.


Authentication Errors:
Verify .env credentials are correct.
Clear browser cookies if session issues occur.



